p.src:
Oblique coordinates add a mixed term. In two dimensions the most general local quadratic form can be written $\begin{aligned}
  ds^2
  &=a_{11}\,dx^2+a_{12}\,dx\,dy\notag\\
  &\quad+a_{21}\,dy\,dx+a_{22}\,dy^2,
  \qquad a_{12}=a_{21}.
  \end{aligned}$ Because ordinary differentials commute, $dx\,dy=dy\,dx$, only the symmetric part of the coefficient array contributes. We collect the coefficients into the matrix $g=
  \begin{pmatrix}
    a_{11} & a_{12}\\
    a_{21} & a_{22}
  \end{pmatrix}.$ This matrix is the metric. It is the local rule that converts coordinate differences into physical intervals.

